 ## Overview
-Ray Marching with Shadow Mapping is a technique for efficient self-shadowing in volume rendering that uses an opacity shadow map to determine occlusion by the volume itself [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of expensive per-view-ray light sampling by precomputing transmittance values from the light's perspective, allowing the renderer to multiply interpolated values from this map by colors from the transfer function during the main pass [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Shadow Volume Rendering](1556-shadow-volume-rendering.md), which explicitly constructs a volumetric shadow buffer, this method uses a 2D depth map combined with a 3D array of opacity images sampled from the light source's perspective [S2, unofficial].
+Ray Marching with Shadow Mapping is a technique for efficient self-shadowing in volume rendering that uses an opacity shadow map to determine occlusion by the volume itself [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of expensive per-view-ray light sampling by precomputing transmittance values from the light's perspective, avoiding the need to trace rays toward the light for each view-ray sample [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Shadow Volume Rendering](1556-shadow-volume-rendering.md), which explicitly constructs a volumetric shadow buffer, this method uses a 2D depth map combined with a 3D array of opacity images sampled from the light source's perspective [S2, unofficial].
 
