 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The light pass requires $O(W \cdot H \cdot N)$ samples, where $W, H$ are shadow map dimensions and $N$ is the number of layers. The main pass requires $O(R \cdot S)$ samples, where $R$ is the number of rays and $S$ is the number of steps per ray. The shadow lookup is $O(1)$ via texture sampling.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The opacity shadow map requires $O(W \cdot H \cdot N)$ memory. For a $512 \times 512$ map with 7 layers, this is significant but manageable [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Measured Performance:** In an example using 28.5 FPS on an AMD Ryzen 7 4700U at 640x480 resolution, 3 deep opacity maps with a 7x512x512 transmittance array were rendered per frame [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** More efficient than sampling light rays towards the light source for each sample of the view ray [S2, unofficial]. Avoids the need for per-view-ray light sampling [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** Visible banding can occur due to the low number of layers [S1, unofficial]. Storing layers in different color channels prevents correct interpolation [S1, unofficial]. If $z_{min}$ is too large, shadows are incorrect [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When self-shadowing is required for volumes and performance is a concern compared to full light sampling.
-*   **When not to use:** When high-quality penumbras are required (consider [Volume Shadow Calculation via Light Strength Buffer](1914-volume-shadow-calculation-via-light-strength-buffer.md)) or when the volume geometry is highly irregular and non-uniform sampling is needed (consider [Deep Opacity Maps](1415-ray-marching-with-shadow-mapping.md) variant).
+*   **When to use:** When self-shadowing is required for volumes and performance is a concern compared to full light sampling [G].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-quality penumbras are required (consider [Volume Shadow Calculation via Light Strength Buffer](1914-volume-shadow-calculation-via-light-strength-buffer.md)) or when the volume geometry is highly irregular and non-uniform sampling is needed (consider [Deep Opacity Maps](1415-ray-marching-with-shadow-mapping.md) variant) [G].
 
 ## Background
-The technique was developed as an alternative to per-view-ray light sampling for volume self-shadowing. It builds on the concept of shadow mapping, extending it to record transparency (transmittance) along different depths from the light's perspective [S1, unofficial]. The term "Opacity Shadow Maps" is used to describe this specific implementation using a 3D texture of transmittance values [S1, unofficial]. Related historical techniques include Deep Shadow Maps by Tom Lokovic and Eric Veach (2000) [S3, unofficial].
